You're certianly right that the color menu is just too big. I haven't
yet implemented a PieMenu parallel of the the 2-dimensional layout
style that LitePullRightMenu supports. (i.e. a /LayoutStyle of [<rows>
<cols>]) It could arrange the labels in layers instead of rows, and
slices instead of columns. The current implementation pushes the menu
radius out until none of the labels overlap, so if you decreased the
size of the labels (i.e. the /colorsquare function), the menu would
probaly be somewhat smaller.

What you could to is to change /usr/NeWS/clientsrc/client/nterm/nterm.ps 
to use LitePullRightMenu instead of DefaultMenu when it creates the
colormenu. The reason the color order is random is that it's getting
the colors by doing a forall on ColorDict, which enumerates them in an
arbirtary order. 

A continuous color wheel pie menu is a good idea. The hue could vary
with the angle, and the brightness with the distance. Another approach
is to have a group of Red, Green, Blue, Hue, Saturation, Brightness
submenus controled just by continuous angular direction, like knobs.
Instead of using the distance parameter to control the color, moving
the cursor further away from the menu center could be used to increase
the accuracy of the directional parameter. Throw in the 2-d
Hue/Brightness menu at the top, and a directional Gray menu at the
bottom, and you've got a complete, reasonably sized, easy to remember
set of color menus.

   Hue/Brightness
    Red  \ /  Hue
 Green  >   <  Saturation
   Blue  / \  Brightness
        Gray

Can't wait till I get a color system! I'm working on a monochrome 3/50
right now, so I haven't had a pressing need to implement this, but
things may change soon!

	-Don


